Next, to investigate the inference performance of the analysed models with respect to the acoustic environment, we create two more subsets: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We re-record all MIDI performances from our MAESTRO subset on a Yamaha Disklavier Enspire ST C1X\footnote{Note that all recordings in the MAESTRO dataset likewise stem from performances on a Disklavier.} using the Focusrite Scarlett 18i8 and a pair of AKG P420 microphones in a moderately bright, fully carpeted room with asymmetric geometry and low background noise level. We use \textit{Disklavier} to refer to this subset.
    % \item We choose a subset from the Batik-plays-Mozart corpus \cite{hu2023batik, hentschel2021annotated} which contains studio recordings on a Bösendorfer 290 SE grand piano. This part of the \textit{evaluation set} will be referred to as \textit{Batik}.
    \item To simulate more challenging real-world environments, we further add perturbations using different levels of reverberation and noise (see Section \ref{sec:ood_inference}). We will refer to this subset as \textit{revnoise}.
\end{enumerate}


We compare three state-of-the-art piano transcription models: Onsets and Frames \cite{hawthorne2019enabling} and the Transformer transcription model \cite{hawthorne2021sequence} by Google/Magenta (which we will refer to as OaF and T5 respectively, in the following), and the high-resolution onset and offset regression model by Bytedance \cite{kong2021highresolution} (referred to as Kong).

We transcribe all the recordings in our MAESTRO, \textit{Disklavier}, and \textit{revnoise} subsets using the (officially provided) trained models, and these transcriptions then form our \emph{\textit{evaluation set}}. 
Note that all audio recordings from the \textit{Disklavier} and \textit{revnoise} subsets are only used for testing; we use the MAESTRO-trained models as they are provided by the respective authors via their repositories.
% We transcribe all our the recordings in our MAESTRO subset, \textit{Disklavier}, \textit{Batik} and \textit{revnoise} using the (officially provided) trained models, and these transcriptions then form our \textit{evaluation set}. 

